# Browser, Context, Page

## 1. Browser

### Definition
Browser is the actual browser instance such as Chrome, Firefox, or Edge.

### Real Life Example
When I open Google Chrome on my laptop, that Chrome application is the Browser.

### Example
- Chrome
- Firefox
- Edge
- Safari

---

## 2. Context

### Definition
A Context is an isolated user session/profile inside a browser.

Each context has its own:
- Cookies
- Login session
- Local storage
- Cache

### Real Life Example

Suppose Chrome has multiple profiles:

- Kirti Profile
- Mridul Profile
- Guest Profile

Each profile has separate login data and settings.

In Playwright, these profiles are similar to Contexts.

### Why Context is Used?

To keep different users' data separate.

Example:
- User A logged in
- User B logged in

Both can work in the same browser without affecting each other.

---

## 3. Page

### Definition
A Page represents a browser tab.

### Real Life Example

Inside Kirti's Chrome profile:

- Amazon tab
- LinkedIn tab
- Instagram tab

Each tab is considered a Page in Playwright.

---

## Hierarchy

Browser
    ↓
Context
    ↓
Page

---

## Complete Real-Life Example

Chrome (Browser)
      │
      ▼
Kirti Profile (Context)
      │
      ├── Amazon Tab (Page 1)
      ├── LinkedIn Tab (Page 2)
      └── Instagram Tab (Page 3)

Mridul Profile (Context)
      │
      └── Gmail Tab (Page 1)